  "title": "O du fröhliche",
  "composer": "Traditional",
  "about": "O du fröhliche means O you joyful, and it is one of the best-known German Christmas songs. Johannes Daniel Falk of Weimar wrote its first verse for the neglected children he took into his home. It was first printed in 1816. Falk set it to O sanctissima, a song to the Virgin Mary first printed in London in 1792.",
  "funFact": "Falk first wrote it for all three great Christian feasts, with one verse for Christmas, one for Easter and one for Pentecost.",
